    ],
    "justification": "Exact branch and endpoint certificates are essential near the zero-slack boundary.",
    "gap": "Generic LP solvers compute values but do not expose this two-score finite certificate or prevent finite-multiplier confusion with infeasibility.",
    "consumer": "A production implementation can certify the early-kill cases \\(\\varepsilon=-0.001,0,0.001\\) without ad hoc tolerances changing the population target.",
    "proof_tex": "For a fixed branch, primal feasibility is a finite linear feasibility problem over \\(|\\mathcal M_a|\\) box variables and two score inequalities.  The slack value is a second finite linear program over \\((x_a,t_a)\\), exactly as displayed in \\(\\text{def:interior-slack}\\).  Thus feasibility and \\(\\tau_{a,b}^r(P)\\) have ordinary primal certificates and, when the numerical inputs are rational, exact rational certificates.\n\nFor a feasible branch, \\(\\text{thm:cone-endpoint-sharpness}\\) shows that the appropriate cone dual is finite and attained.  Each summand in either dual switches affine formula only when\n\\[\n 1-b\\lambda's_j^r=0\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n 1+b\\lambda's_j^r=0,                                                           \\tag{42}\n\\]\nrespectively.  Together with \\(\\lambda_1=0\\) and \\(\\lambda_2=0\\), these lines partition \\(\\mathbb R_+^2\\) into finitely many polyhedral cells.  On the closure of every cell the dual objective is affine.  Every such cell is pointed because it is contained in the nonnegative quadrant.  If a finite affine optimum occurs in a cell interior, either a direction improves the value until a boundary is reached or the objective is constant in that direction; repeating in two dimensions reaches a vertex without changing the optimum.  If the maximizing or minimizing face is an unbounded ray, finiteness rules out improvement along the ray, and moving to its finite endpoint again gives a vertex optimum.  Every vertex is the nonnegative intersection of two linearly independent boundary lines, with the origin included.  Hence \\(\\mathsf A_{\\mathrm{arr}}\\) returns the same value as the dual and therefore the primal LP.\n\nWith \\(m=|\\mathcal M_a|\\), there are at most \\(m+2\\) relevant lines and hence \\(O(m^2)\\) pairs.  Evaluating a dual objective takes \\(O(m)\\) arithmetic operations, giving \\(O(m^3)\\) naive arithmetic per endpoint.  Parallel lines simply yield no linearly independent intersection; a zero score creates no kink because its summand is globally affine; tied optima are harmless because only one optimal vertex is needed.\n\nFor completeness, infeasibility has a distinct certificate.  Let\n\\(G=\\{b(h_a^r+\\sum_jp_js_j^rx_j):x\\in\\prod_j[\\ell_a,u_a]\\}\\).  This compact convex set is disjoint from \\(\\mathbb R_+^2\\) exactly when the branch is empty.  Choose closest points \\(g_0\\in G\\) and \\(z_0\\in\\mathbb R_+^2\\), and put \\(\\lambda=z_0-g_0\\).  The two projection inequalities give\n\\[\n \\lambda\\ge0,\\qquad \\lambda'z_0=0,\\qquad\n \\sup_{g\\in G}\\lambda'g\\le-\\|z_0-g_0\\|^2<0.                                 \\tag{43}\n\\]\nScaling \\(\\lambda\\) in the lower dual sends its value to \\(+\\infty\\), while scaling it in the upper dual sends its value to \\(-\\infty\\).  Thus (43) is a recession certificate of emptiness and cannot be confused with failure to find a finite multiplier.  Finally, structural-zero cells are absent from \\(\\mathcal J\\) by \\(\\text{ass:finite-grid}\\); the preceding arguments require neither general position nor unique optima.  This proves all asserted degeneracy cases and complexity bounds.",
